Fix ImGui overlay window-content flicker on GL3Plus/Mesa.

OGRE's ImGuiOverlay keeps ONE shared dynamic vertex/index buffer and only
reallocates it when it needs to grow, reusing it across frames otherwise. When
the CPU runs ahead of the GPU the next frame's writeData() overwrites a buffer
the GPU is still reading from the previous frame's draw, so windows drawn later
in the frame lose their geometry -- seen as flicker/blanking of ImGui content.

Allocating a fresh buffer every frame and freeing the old one immediately is not
enough on Mesa: once a frame limiter paces the loop, the driver's buffer pool
has time to recycle the just-freed memory into the next "fresh" allocation,
aliasing a draw still being scanned out -- the flicker returns whenever the fps
cap is on. Instead allocate fresh AND retain the last few buffers in a ring, so
their memory stays allocated (not pooled) until the GPU is long done with them.
The fix is then independent of frame pacing. Buffers are small (tens of KB).

--- a/Components/Overlay/src/OgreImGuiOverlay.cpp
+++ b/Components/Overlay/src/OgreImGuiOverlay.cpp
@@ -374,17 +374,26 @@ void ImGuiOverlay::ImGUIRenderable::updateVertexData(ImDrawData* draw_data)

     VertexBufferBinding* bind = mRenderOp.vertexData->vertexBufferBinding;

-    if (bind->getBindings().empty() || bind->getBuffer(0)->getNumVertices() < size_t(draw_data->TotalVtxCount))
-    {
-        bind->setBinding(0, HardwareBufferManager::getSingleton().createVertexBuffer(
-                                sizeof(ImDrawVert), draw_data->TotalVtxCount, HBU_CPU_TO_GPU));
-    }
-    if (!mRenderOp.indexData->indexBuffer ||
-        mRenderOp.indexData->indexBuffer->getNumIndexes() < size_t(draw_data->TotalIdxCount))
-    {
-        mRenderOp.indexData->indexBuffer = HardwareBufferManager::getSingleton().createIndexBuffer(
-            HardwareIndexBuffer::IT_16BIT, draw_data->TotalIdxCount, HBU_CPU_TO_GPU);
-    }
+    // PSX engine patch: allocate a fresh vertex+index buffer every frame AND
+    // retain the last few in a ring, so the buffer the GPU is still reading (or
+    // scanning out) is never freed and its memory can't be pooled back into the
+    // next frame's "fresh" allocation. Reusing one buffer, or freeing it
+    // immediately, let the CPU or the driver's buffer pool alias a draw still in
+    // flight -> ImGui window-content flicker on GL3Plus/Mesa, notably once a
+    // frame limiter paces the loop so the pool has time to recycle the memory.
+    static const int kRing = 4;
+    static HardwareVertexBufferSharedPtr vbRing[kRing];
+    static HardwareIndexBufferSharedPtr  ibRing[kRing];
+    static int ringPos = 0;
+    ringPos = (ringPos + 1) % kRing;
+
+    HardwareVertexBufferSharedPtr vb = HardwareBufferManager::getSingleton().createVertexBuffer(
+        sizeof(ImDrawVert), draw_data->TotalVtxCount, HBU_CPU_TO_GPU);
+    HardwareIndexBufferSharedPtr ib = HardwareBufferManager::getSingleton().createIndexBuffer(
+        HardwareIndexBuffer::IT_16BIT, draw_data->TotalIdxCount, HBU_CPU_TO_GPU);
+    vbRing[ringPos] = vb; // retained kRing frames so its memory can't be realiased
+    ibRing[ringPos] = ib;
+    bind->setBinding(0, vb);
+    mRenderOp.indexData->indexBuffer = ib;

     // Copy all vertices
     size_t vtx_offset = 0;
